      "overview": "In this video we discuss i’rāb and binā. Iʿrāb is the grammatical change at the end of words caused by governing agents (‘awāmil). It clarifies meaning and shows a word’s role in the sentence. It may be visible (like in زيدٌ / زيدًا) or assumed (like الفتى). Scholars differ whether iʿrāb is the change itself (maʿnawī) or the resulting sign (lafẓī). It exists to distinguish overlapping meanings and roles of words using light markers—mainly the ḥarakāt. Its four types are rafʿ, naṣb, khafḍ (or jarr), and jazm: nouns take the first three, verbs the last three.\nBināʾ means a word’s ending remains fixed regardless of ʿawāmil. It is defined as always keeping a set vowel or sukūn (ḍamm, fatḥ, kasr, or sukūn). Unlike iʿrāb, its stability comes not from grammar but from its nature, usage, or structure. The default in bināʾ is sukūn, though opinions differ on terminology.",
