      "overview": "In this video, we discuss the ġayr munṣarif ism. Man‘ al-ṣarf prevents an ism from showing tanwīn or kasrah due to its resemblance to the fi‘l. Adding alif-lām or iḍāfah removes this resemblance. Instead of kasrah, it takes fatḥah in jarr.\nThere are nine causes: jam‘, wazn al-fi‘l, ‘adl, ta’nīth, ta‘rīf, tarkīb, alif+nūn, ‘ujmah, and waṣf. A word becomes ġayr munṣarif if it has two causes or one strong cause. Three causes equal two on their own: jam‘ al-muntahā (like مساجد, مصابيح), ta’nīth with alif maqṣūrah, and ta’nīth with alif mamdūdah.\nWazn al-fi‘l applies when a proper noun takes:\nA verb-only pattern (ضُرِب, استخرج, أحمد).\n\nA pattern mostly for verbs (افعل).\n\nA shared pattern where the fi‘l has a stronger claim (أكتب vs أكلب).\n\nCombined with waṣfiyyah, it affects adjectives:\nṢifah mushabbahah on أفعل (أحمر, أعرج, أدعج).\n\nIsm al-tafḏīl (أحسن).",
