      "portalId": "11001850-f82b-460d-9df1-51081cc16ccd",
      "title": "Lesson 25 : The Indicative Noun and Relative Noun",
      "overview": "In this video we discuss the ism al-ishārah and ism al-mawṣūl. Ism al-Ishārah points to what is indicated, e.g., ذا (masc. sg.), ذي/ذه/تي/تا (fem. sg.), ذان/ذَين (masc. dual), تان/تَين (fem. dual), and أُولاء (plural, with or without madd). Levels: qarīb (هذا), baʿīd (ذلك), and middle (ذاك). Add hā for tanbīh, kāf for distance, lām for emphasis of distance. For places: هنا for near, هناك/هنالك/ثم for far. All are mabnī.\nIsm Mawṣūl needs a ṣilah and a pronoun return, making it maʿrifah. Used to let sentences act as adjectives (e.g., الذي يمشي). Divided into naṣṣ (specific forms: الذي، التي، اللذان/اللذين، اللتان/اللتين، الذين، اللائي/اللاتي/اللواتي) and mushtarak (من، ما). Found in Qur’ān as mubtada, mafʿūl, or muḍāf ilayh.",
