      "portalId": "a06286fc-6094-4d38-a4c9-a46ac55f3e54",
      "title": "Lesson 28 : The Ism made Definite by the Alif and Lām",
      "overview": "In this video, we discuss the alif lām and anything muḏāf to a ma’rifah word. The word المعرَّف بالأداة refers to a noun made definite by alif-lām, which has three types:\nZāidah (extra): The noun is definite without it.\n\n – Lāzimah: Fixed from the time the word is coined (السؤال، الذي، المدينة).\n\n – Ġayr lāzimah: Added later for poetry or emphasis (وطبت النفس، الحسن).\n\nMawṣūlah: When ال acts as an ism mawṣūl.\n\nMuʿarrifah (defining):\n\n – ʿAhdiyyah: Refers to something known — ḍikriyyah (previously mentioned), ḍihniyyah (mentally known), or ḥuḏūriyyah (present).\n\n – Jinsiyyah: Refers to a class or essence — māhiyyah (essence), istiġrāq (all members), or ṣifāt al-afrād (traits of a kind).\n\nScholars differ in whether only the lām is for ta’rīf or it's both the hamzah and the lām. In Ḥimyarī Arabic, the lām changes to a mīm.\nAnything muḏāf to a ma’rifah becomes ma’rifah like غلامي، غلام زيد، غلام هذا، غلام الذي قام أبوه، غلام الرجل, unless it’s inherently vague like غير and مثل.",
      "topic": ""
