      "portalId": "8f638ff7-e59c-4884-aa24-5ecf343bf0e1",
      "title": "Lesson 31 : The Mubtada (Subject) and Khabar (Predicate)",
      "overview": "The word المبتدأ is a marfūʿ noun free from any explicit governing agent. It’s usually marfūʿ unless preceded by an extra ḥarf al-jarr (e.g. ليس زيدٌ بقائمٍ). Sometimes it can be majrūr lafẓan but marfūʿ maḥallan, like وما من إلهٍ غيرُ الله.\nThe mubtada is of two types: ẓāhir (explicit noun) and muḍmar (pronoun). The ẓāhir is either:\nOne that has a khabar, e.g. اللّٰهُ ربُّنا.\n\nOne whose marfūʿ word replaces the khabar, e.g. أقائمٌ زيدٌ؟. This occurs with ism fāʿil or ism mafʿūl after negation or interrogation.\n\nThe mubtada cannot be nakirah unless justified — such as being preceded by negation (ما رجلٌ قائمٌ), interrogation (هل رجلٌ جالسٌ), being mawṣūf (ولعبدٌ مؤمنٌ خيرٌ من مشركٍ), muḍāf (خمسُ صلواتٍ كتبهن الله), or when the khabar is a ẓarf or jār–majrūr that comes first (عندك رجلٌ). It may also be a verbal noun clause (وأن تصوموا خيرٌ لكم).\nThe word الخبر completes the meaning of the mubtada. It is of two types:\nMufrad – a single word (زيدٌ قائمٌ).\n\nNon-mufrad – either:\n\nJumlah ismiyyah, e.g. زيد جاريتُه ذاهبةٌ\n\nJumlah fiʿliyyah, e.g. زيد قام أبوهُ\n\nShibh al-jumlah, like زيد في الدارِ or عندك رجلٌ.",
      "topic": ""
