      "portalId": "1a66b075-56ed-4442-82d5-279c58801c7d",
      "title": "Lesson 33 : Kāna and its Sisters",
      "overview": "In this video we discuss the agents acting on the mubtada and khabar, called nawāsikh, includes three types:\nThose that raise the mubtada and make the khabar mansūb, like كان and its sisters, the ḥurūf resembling ليس, and the verbs of nearness.\n\nThose that make the mubtada mansūb and raise the khabar, like إنّ and its sisters and the لا of universal negation.\n\nThose that make both mansūb, like ظنّ and its sisters.\n\nكان and its sisters raise the mubtada (their ism) like a fā‘il and make the khabar mansūb like a maf‘ūl bihi.\nThey are three groups:\nThose that act without a condition: كان، أمسى، أصبح، ظل، بات، صار، ليس.\n\nThose that require negation, prohibition, or supplication before them: زال، فتئ، برح، انفك.\n\nThose that need ما maṣdariyyah ẓarfiyyah before them: دام, as in ما دمت حيًّا.\n\nThe khabar may come between the verb and its ism (وكان حقًا علينا نصر المؤمنين) or before the verb except in ليس and دام, e.g. عالماً كان زيد.\nAll their forms — past, present, command, verbal noun, and active participle — perform the same grammatical function (حتى يكونوا مؤمنين).\nThey can also be tāmm (complete verbs) meaning “to occur or happen,” like وإن كان ذو عسرة (if one is in hardship), except زال، فتئ، ليس, which remain nāqiṣ.",
