      "overview": "In this video, we discuss Ishtighāl, the munādā and the maf’ūl muṭlaq. Ishtighāl is when a noun comes first, and the verb after it is occupied with acting on its pronoun (or something connected to it), so it cannot act on the noun directly.\nExamples: زيدًا اضربه, زيدًا أنا ضاربه, زيدًا ضربتُ غلامه, and {وكلَّ إنسانٍ ألزمناه طائره}.\nIn all of these, the naṣb of the first noun is caused by a necessary omitted verb, explained by the verb that follows.\nSo the meaning is: اضرب زيدًا اضربه, أنا ضارب زيدًا أنا ضاربه, أهنتُ زيدًا ضربت غلامه, وألزمنا كل إنسان ألزمناه.\n\nThe munādā (the called person) originally comes from a hidden verb like أدعو, replaced by يا.\nIt has five types: the singular ‘alam, the intended indefinite, the non-intended indefinite, the muḍāf, and what resembles the muḍāf.\nThe ‘alam and the intended indefinite are mabnī on what they would be given the sign of raf'\n\nWhen the munādā is muḍāf to yāʾ al-mutakallim, six forms are allowed:\nDropping the yāʾ and using kasrah: يا عبادِ (most common)\n\nThe maf‘ūl muṭlaq is a maṣdar used to:\n– emphasize its verb,\n– clarify its type, or\n– clarify its number.\nExamples: {وكلم الله موسى تكليمًا}, ضربت زيدًا ضربًا شديدًا, ضربته ضربتين, {فدكتا دكةً واحدةً}.\nIt can match the verb in wording (لفظي) or only in meaning (معنوي), as in جلست قعودًا and قمت وقوفًا.\nThe maṣdar is the noun of an action, usually the third form of a verb: ضرب يضرب ضربًا.\nThings besides a maṣdar can take its place as maf‘ūl muṭlaq, such as:\n– كلَّ and بعضَ with a maṣdar: {فلا تميلوا كل الميل}\n– numbers: {فاجلدوهم ثمانين جلدةً}\n– tool-nouns: ضربته سوطًا، عصًا.",
