      "title": "Lesson 43 : The Ḥāl Continued",
      "overview": "In this video, we complete our discussion on the ḥāl. The ḥāl only comes after a complete clause—not as one of its essential parts. This doesn’t mean the sentence is complete without it; in ولا تمشِ في الأرض مرحًا, مرحًا is a maṣdar used as ḥāl and understood as an ism fā‘il.\nThe ḍūl-ḥāl is normally ma‘rifah, except when the ḥāl comes before it (جاء ماشيًا رجلٌ), when the noun has some specification (طالبٌ مجتهدٌ or طالبُ علمٍ), or when it follows negation or questioning. It can also be nakirah when justified, such as في الدار جالسًا رجلٌ, or like سواءً للسائلين, لها منذرون, or مصدّقًا in certain readings.\nThe ḥāl may appear as a ẓarf (بين السحاب) or jār–majrūr (في زينته), linked to an implied مستقر. It can also be a sentence, attached with wāw and a pronoun (وهم ألوف), a pronoun alone (عدوّ), or just wāw (ونحن عصبة). The wāw may be required, omitted, or optional depending on context.\nAmong its types: temporary vs. permanent (راكبًا / ضعيفًا), the ḥāl as the main point (ضاحكًا) or leading to another meaning (بشرًا سويًّا), occurring with the action, narrated from the past, or intended for the future (خالدين). It can clarify or emphasize, and may come as one or more ḥāls, either nested or parallel, as in راكبًا متبسمًا.",
