      "title": "Lesson 45 : The Mustathnā",
      "overview": "The agents of istithnā’ are: illā (a ḥarf), ghayr and siwā (isms with dialects سِوى، سُوى، سِواء، سِواء), laysa and lā yakūn (verbs), and khalā, ‘adā, and ḥāshā (either verbs giving naṣb or particles giving jarr). Some forms of ḥāshā are also used for tanzīh, as in {حاش لله}.\nIstithnā’ is mūjab (affirmative) like جاء القوم إلا زيدًا, or ghayr mūjab (negation, prohibition, questioning) like ما جاء القوم إلا زيدٌ/زيدًا. It is tāmm when the mustathnā minhu is mentioned and nāqiṣ/mufarraġ when omitted, as in ما قام إلا زيدٌ. It is muttaṣil when the mustathnā belongs to the group and munqaṭi‘ when it does not.\nWith illā:\n– In tāmm mūjab, the mustathnā is manṣūb (قام القوم إلا زيدًا).\n– In tāmm ghayr mūjab, naṣb or badal are both allowed; badal is preferred in muttaṣil like {ما فعلوه إلا قليلٌ منهم}.\n– In munqaṭi‘, naṣb is preferred (قام القوم إلا حمارًا).\n– In nāqiṣ/mufarraġ, the mustathnā takes whatever i‘rāb the governing word requires: ما رأيت إلا زيدًا، وما مررت إلا بزيد.\nWith ghayr and siwā, the mustathnā is majrūr by iḍāfah, and ghayr/siwā take the i‘rāb that the mustathnā of illā would have had (قاموا غيرَ زيدٍ). I‘rāb is visible when mamdūdah and estimated when maqṣūrah.\nWith laysa and lā yakūn, the mustathnā is always manṣūb (قام القوم ليس زيدًا).\nWith khalā, ‘adā, and ḥāshā, both jarr and naṣb are possible. If jarr is used, they are particles; if naṣb, they are verbs. According to Sībawayh, ḥāshā is only heard with jarr. When mā enters ‘adā or khalā, naṣb becomes obligatory (ما عدا زيدًا).\nThe chapter closes by noting that issues like the khabar of kāna, the ism of inna, and the khabar of the near-action verbs were already discussed previously.",
