      "title": "Lesson 48 : The I’rāb of the Fi’l and the particles of Naṣb",
      "overview": "The fiʿl is of three types: māḍī, amr, and muḍāriʿ.\nThe māḍī and amr are mabnī, and only the muḍāriʿ is muʿrab unless it has nūn al-niswah or nūn al-tawkīd, in which case it becomes mabnī.\nThe muḍāriʿ is marfūʿ by default until a nāṣib gives it naṣb or a jāzim gives it jazm.\nExample: إيّاك نعبدُ وإيّاك نستعينُ.\nThere are four nāṣibs that give naṣb directly:\n– أَن (maṣdariyyah, mukhaffafah, tafsīriyyah, zā’idah) unless preceded by علم or ظن.\n– لَن for emphasis (not eternity).\n– كَيْ maṣdariyyah.\n– إِذَنْ when its conditions are met (beginning of speech, future verb, direct attachment).\nThere are five nāṣibs that need a hidden أَن:\n– Lām of كي (taʿlīl, ʿāqibah, zā’idah).\n– Wāw, fā’, thumma, or aw when doing ʿaṭf on a pure noun/maṣdar.\n– And poetic examples showing correct usage.\nThere are six nāṣibs that require hidden أن necessarily:\n– كي as a particle of jarr.\n– Lām al-juḥūd after a negated kāna.\n– حَتّى with its three scenarios (necessary naṣb, optional, necessary rafʿ).\n– أَوْ meaning “إلى” or “إلّا”.\n– Fāʾ al-sababiyyah.\n– Wāw al-maʿiyyah.\nBoth groups (those with optional hidden أن and those with necessary hidden أن) must be preceded by pure negation or a request such as amr, nahy, duʿā, tarjī, istifhām, etc.\nExamples:\nلا يُقضى عليهم فيموتوا\nولا تطغوا فيه فيحلَّ عليكم غضبي\nلا تأكل السمك وتشربَ اللبن.\nA final note: after lām al-taʿlīl, if the verb is attached to lā, then أن must be explicitly mentioned to avoid two consecutive lāms:\nلِئَلّا يعلمَ أهلُ الكتاب.",
