      "overview": "In this video, we discuss the na’t as one of the four tawābi’ mentioned by Ibn Mālik (النعت، التوكيد، العطف، البدل), all of which follow the first noun in i‘rāb.\nThe text clarifies that the na’t is a tābi’ which must be either an ism mushtaqq (such as the ism fā‘il, ism maf‘ūl, ṣifah mushabbahah, ism mubālaghah, or ism tafḍīl) or something interpreted as a mushtaqq (like اسم الإشارة, اسم الموصول, ذو بمعنى صاحب, and nisbah adjectives). It can also come as a jumlah or maṣdar, each with specific conditions—such as the man‘ūt being nakirah for jumlah, and the maṣdar remaining singular and masculine.\nThe chapter divides the na‘t into two primary types:\nNa‘t Ḥaqīqī – which directly describes the man‘ūt. It contains an implied pronoun referring back to the man‘ūt and must follow it in:\n- i‘rāb (raf‘/naṣb/jarr),\n- definiteness,\n- gender,\n- amount.\n\nNa‘t Sababī – which describes something connected to the man‘ūt (like his father or mother). Here, the ism connected to the man‘ūt becomes the fā‘il or nā’ib al-fā‘il of the ṣifah. It follows the man‘ūt only in definiteness and i‘rāb, while the ṣifah itself agrees with the fā‘il in gender. It is usually kept singular, except in the language of أكلوني البراغيث and in cases involving broken plurals, where grammarians like Sībawayh prefer using the broken plural.",
