      "overview": "In this lesson, we discuss the ism al-fiʿl, which combines features of both noun and verb. The Kūfiyyūn regard it as a verb, while the Baṣriyyūn describe it as a noun carrying the meaning of a verb. It appears in three forms: murtajil (originally coined for this use, like صه and مَه), manqūl (shifted from another construction like عليك), and qiyāsī (derived regularly from certain verbs on the pattern فَعالِ). It behaves like the verb whose meaning it carries, governs its maʿmūl, does not form iḍāfah, and its maʿmūl may not precede it—except in the debated Qurʾānic case {كتابَ الله عليكم}. When it has tanwīn, it is nakirah, and without tanwīn it is maʿrifah.\nWe then introduce tanāzuʿ, where two or more governing agents precede one or more maʿmūls, and each seeks governance over the delayed element, such as in {آتُوني أُفْرِغْ عليه قِطْرًا}. Both schools allow either agent to govern, but the Baṣriyyūn prefer the closer agent, while the Kūfiyyūn prefer the earlier one.",
